---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const cellCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '1.1 - 0.34*log(x)', label: 'cathode (reduction) potential', color: '#1f6f54', width: 2 },
    { expr: '-0.76 + 0.34*log(x)', label: 'anode (oxidation) potential', color: '#d9480f', width: 2 },
    { expr: '1.1 - 0.34*log(x) - (-0.76 + 0.34*log(x))', label: 'cell voltage = difference', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.001, max: 10, label: 'ion concentration (mol/L), log scale' },
  y: { min: -1.2, max: 1.4, label: 'potential (V)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/redox-and-electrochemistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Redox</strong> reactions transfer electrons: one substance is oxidised (loses
    electrons), another is reduced (gains them — remember OIL RIG). Run the two halves in
    separate beakers, connect them with a wire and a salt bridge, and the forced electron journey
    becomes an <strong>electric current</strong> — the battery, invented by Volta in 1800,
    reimagined by every phone you own.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a cell's voltage is the difference between its two half-reactions — concentration shifts both, and the cell drains" widget="plotter" config={cellCfg} />

  <p>
    The blue curve is what a voltmeter reads between a copper cathode (+0.34 V standard) and a
    zinc anode (−0.76 V): about 1.1 V fresh. As the battery discharges, concentrations drift and
    the Nernst equation drags the curves toward each other — dead battery = no difference in
    pulling power. Rechargeable cells simply reverse the current to un-drain them.
  </p>

  <h2>The working concepts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oxidation states</strong> are the bookkeeping: assign charges as if ionic, and any change flags electron transfer. Rusting is iron 0 → +3; photosynthesis is carbon +4 → 0 — biology's biggest redox.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard potentials rank pulling power.</strong> Fluorine (+2.87 V) rips electrons from almost anything; lithium (−3.04 V) surrenders them eagerly — which is exactly why lithium sits at the heart of the best batteries.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrolysis runs it backwards:</strong> push current in to force non-spontaneous reactions — aluminium from bauxite, chlorine from brine, hydrogen from water (the green-fuel question is an energy price question).</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosion is electrochemistry in the wild:</strong> a wet iron nail is a short-circuited battery eating itself. Sacrificial zinc anodes on ships corrode on purpose so the hull doesn't.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Volta (1800); Nernst equation (1889); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Electrochemistry).</p>
</TopicPage>
